Every place that I have worked has had a policy *against*
buying any software that is keyed to the Ethernet hardware
address.   It causes too many problems, such as the time
a CPU board (a VAX 3400, which has the ethernet controller
on-board) was replaced on a Friday evening before a 4-day
weekend.   The one piece of mission-critical software that
would not come up was keyed to the ethernet address, and the
vendor was closed for four days.   Critical system functions
simply could not be performed.

Are you prepared to deal with the lawsuits resulting from
such occurances?   I suggest that you have a chat with your
lawyer and your insurance company before you embark on such
a path.

