In article <4bptio$e2p@nova.avid.com>, Douglas Thurlow <douglas_thurlow@avid.com> writes:

>I am trying to put a Seagate Barracuda ST-15150 drive on a DS5000/133 
>running Ultrix 4.4.  But newfs bails out before it's finished.   I 
>edited /etc/disktab to adjust the parameters there for an rz29 drive for 
>the ST15150.  Obviously, this doesn't work too well.
>
>Does anyone know a better way to do this?  Can it be done?  Can you 
>suggest a disktab entry that would work, or the parameters to newfs to 
>initialize an ST15150?

ULTRIX has a 2 GB partition size limit (4,194,303 sectors).  If the
drive is larger than this, then you'll need to create a small file
system on the A partition to write a partition table.  Then you can
use chpt(8) to carve out partitions of suitable size.  Newfs can get
the geometry information from the drive and will use whatever partition
table is defined on the drive.  So, you don't really need a disktab
entry.  I don't think partition size definitions in disktab will be
of any use, since there isn't a way to specify the partition starting
LBN.

If you know how you want to size the first partition, you can use
the -s option of newfs to create the first one, and then use chpt(8)
to set any others:

newfs -s 65536 /dev/rrz#a st15150

or:

newfs -s 4194303 /dev/rrz#c st15150

A support 2+ GB disk, the RZ74, makes the C partition the limit and
then uses another for the remainder of the disk.
>

