> A friend of mine recently inherited a DEC VAX from a long long time
> ago with the number 11/725 promiently displayed on the front panel.
> The machine came with no console, no cable that looks as though it
> could go to a console and no documentation whatsoever.  It has what
> appears to be a removable hard drive as well as two tape drives, one
> on the front panel, one on the side.  I suspect there is an regular
> fixed disk buried in there somewhere as well.  Just in case you
> haven't noticed I know next to nothing about DEC machines, but all the
> same, we'd like to see if we can't get this thing to boot (or at least
> display SOMETHING on a console).  Can anyone give me any guidance on
> what needs to be connected to the console port on the back end of the
> thing, and what needs to be done to start the boot process?  I would
> certainly appriciate any help at all!
> 
> 
> Thanks much!
> 
> Bill Ehrman, Jr.
> 

	Man does that ever take me back!!  A long time ago (when I was an 
eager grad student) we bought a VAX 11/725 when they first came out.  They 
were essentially a repackage of the 11/730 borad set.  At least it used the 
same CPU, but I think it had some restriction on the amount of memory you 
could stuff into the unibus.
	Anyhow, the standard configuration of this machine was a pair of 
TU58 QIC cartridge drives, and a weird combo removable/fixed hard disk.
For the life of me, I can't remember the name of the thing right now, but 
I do remember it ran from a strange interface board called a KLESI controller.
(maybe it was something like DZ25?)  Anyhow, the drive had a very short 
product life and I don't think that very many were sold.  The cartridges 
were kind of expensive (~$300 each ?) and were of a very limited capacity.
The thing had a fixed platter inside which held 26 Mbytes, and the removable 
cartridge also held 26 Mbytes for a grand total of 52 Mbytes.  Now that I 
start to think, I believe that the system memory in the 725 was limited to 
something like 5 Mbytes (1Mbyte on the CPU, and only one 4Mbyte board).  
I may be wrong about that, but anyhow...
	The machine uses a standard VT100 (or better) for a console device.
There should be a separate DB25 plug for that.  Now for the bad news.  The 
11/725 CPU (and I assume the 11/730) were micro-coded CPUs where the 
micro-code was stored in RAM memory!!  In theory this was to allow the nice 
people at DEC to send you a CPU upgrade on one of those cute little TU58 
cartridges.  In practice, it didn't happen!  The upshot here is that if you 
don't have the micro-code tape cartridge (it should go in the slot on the 
side of the box if you do have it), You've pretty much got an ugly coffee 
table on wheels!  If you do have it, when you plug everything in, and turn 
everything on, you should get a prompt like >>>  to which you reply 
something like b or boot, and then if VMS resides on the fixed platter, VMS 
comes up.  Good luck!  I think you'll probably need it!!   8-{).

	If any of this is wrong, flame away!



