In article <4c0uik$al8@cicg-communication.grenet.fr>, hewat@ill.fr (Alan Hewat) writes:
> What is the command to get the unique system identification number for
> a DEC alpha running Unix ?  running openVMS ? I have done 'man -k' and
> looked through the FAQ without sucess.

There is NO unique system identification number.  The closest you can get is
the Ethernet hardware address, and this mechanism will fail if your customer is
forced to replace his controller.

> I need this ID number for a simple software licensing program.

Use Digital's LMF.  That's what it's intended for, what system managers expect,
and what some decision makers will *demand*.  Hardware-based licensing
protection is archaic, and I personally find it insulting and limiting.  I
can't incorporate my disaster recovery plan with a lot of these types of
schemes and it means that before I can recover my services to my users, I have
to contact my vendors to get new keys.  With the LMF, I don't have to do this.

