On the RSX86B sigtape, I found a file FPPEMULAT.MAC which begins
like this:

	.TITLE FPP
	.IDENT	/V1.1/
;FIS FLOATING POINT EMULATOR
;
;WRITTEN 15-DEC-78 BY DANIEL STEINBERG
;                       SRI INTERNATIONAL  loc K1023
;                       415 326-6200 ext.5539
;
; MODIFIED 8-MAR-79 TO CORRECT LDF,LDCDF,LDCIF INPUT BYTE COUNT ERROR
;
;
;The basic idea is this:
; On the PDP 11/40 or 11/03 with FIS (Floating Instruction Set), the FP11
; floating point processor op-codes are illegal instructions.  RSX-11M
; provides a mechanism whereby tasks may be notified, via an SST (Synchronous
; System Trap), when an illegal instruction is encountered.  The task is then
; free to handle it as it wishes, without further Executive interference.
; This emulator is entered at FPPSST when illegal instructions occur.  If the
; offending op-code is not a floating point op-code, then the emulator removes
; its entry point from the SST vector table and returns to attempt to execute
; the instruction again.  This time, either the task itself will field the
; instruction, or the Executive will abort the task.  If the offending op-code
; is a legal FPP instruction code, the emulator will decode and execute it,
; using the FIS instructions.

Is this what you were thinking of?  I've put the entire file
at the anonymous ftp site sunsite.unc.edu, in the directory

/pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/rsx/fpp

if anyone's interested.

Incidentally, I'm just about ready to put the source and binary
codes from many of the RSX sigtapes at sunsite.unc.edu.  Many
object code files will also be there, but will probably be useless
as Unix can't preserve the file characteristics properly.  This is
a many-hundreds-of-megabytes task, and there will undoubtedly be
some problems (not the least of which is the incredible ftp load
already on sunsite), but I hope it'll be useful.

Tim. (shoppa@altair.krl.caltech.edu)



