In article <30D9EB9C.3A17@datatel.com>, Lee Sweet <lee@datatel.com> writes:
> I *know* this has probably been beaten to death here and elsewhere, so 
> either point me to a FAQ or just a short answer.  I always thought that 
> '50 drives couldn't do anything (at least not legally) to a '70 tape.  
> Now I'm told by co-workers that 'DEC says' that a 50 drive can write to 
> a 70 tape at 50 density. Yes or no?  Thanks much!

        Yes, but with caveats.  The TK70 (TK52?) tape must not have been
    initialized by a TK70 drive.  If it has, you can still use it (write
    to it) if you  first  degauss  it.   The  reverse  is also true as I
    recently found out.  Take a TK50 software distribution tape, load it
    into  a  TK70, and try to build, e.g., standalone backup on it.   No
    go!  You need to first degauss the tape.  The reason  (according  to
    an  old  post  by  Jerry  Leichter)  is  that  the drives write some
    formatting information at the beginning  of  the tape, and the TK70,
    at  least, refuses to write to a tape that's been formatted for  low
    density TK50 use.  A safety feature of some  sort?  :-(   Degaussing
    the tape did the trick though.  :-)

        Actually,  since  I've  only  done  this  last  operation,  it's
    entirely  possible that a TK50 doesn't check the formatting and will
    just overwrite the TK70 tape.  I simply have no knowledge one way or
    the other whether that works.  A blank or degaussed tape is  certain
    to work.

        -Ken
