Excerpts from netnews.comp.sys.dec: 21-Dec-95 W/NT on Ultra Hinote? by
Dirk Grunwald@foobar.cs. 
> Has anyone tried running W/NT for Workstations 3.51 on a DEC Ultra
> Hinote?
>  
I just tried this on my new Ultra and it seemed to work (more or less)
properly.

>         + PCMCIA support?
>
No problems here, it recognized my modem and ethernet card just fine.

>         + Power management support?
>  
As already mentioned in another response, this is not there yet. 
Suspend/Resume works fine as does "go-to-sleep-after-5 min.-idle", but
even the (hardware??) battery indicator at the corner of the screen is
incorrect (always seems to show a full battery, even when I know this
isn't right).

The only other thing I had trouble with is the video driver support -
the default leaves me with 16-color VGA and NT doesn't seem to want to
understand the video driver disk created with Make Master Disks.

My final gripe is with disk compression software (nothing to do
specifically with the Ultra) and I think this is going to keep me off NT
for a bit.  I have a 500 MB drive, but space is still at a premium
(isn't it always...), which means I'd like to compress things like the
25-40 MB per-application Office products which I know have a lot of big
files I only use now-and-then anyhow.  Anyhow, NT doesn't recognize
DriveSpace drives and using NTFS would require me to convert the entire
disk which I can't do because I still need DOS/WfW for one or two other
programs that will not work in NT.  This means there is no way to keep
Word from taking up *all that space*.  I think I'll give W95 a spin
(been meaning to anyhow) over the holidays and see how that turns out.

Good luck.

Erik Riedel
School of Computer Science
Carnegie Mellon University




