In article <danpop.818990982@rscernix> danpop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>First, basic C knowledge :-)
>
>Your conclusion is incorrect and it is based on broken code, which 
>invokes undefined behaviour, hence it cannot be used to prove anything
>even if it happens to "work".
>
>The correct way to display pointer values is to use %p and to cast the
>pointers to void *, because this is what %p expects.  %lx expects an
>unsigned long, not a pointer of whatever size.  So, the correct fprintf
>calls are:
>
>    fprintf(stderr,"woof = 0x%p\n", (void *)woof);
>    fprintf(stderr,"block = 0x%p\n", (void *)block);
...

ANSI X3J11/88-159 says on p. 135 of �4.9.6.1 (The fprintf function):

"
p	The argument shall be a pointer to void.  The value of the pointer is
	converted to a sequence of printable characters, in an
	implementation-defined manner.			 ^^^^^
"	^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

The only use defined by ANSI for a string fprintf'ed with %p is to fscanf it
with %p during the same program execution.  fprintf %p could encrypt a pointer
value to a string using a one-time pad, and as long as fscanf %p was able to
decrypt it, that would be a valid implementation.
				/AHM
