In article <4b4ms7$r5s@mozo.cc.purdue.edu>, young@dynamo.ecn.purdue.edu (Mike Young) writes:
:	Greetings. I have fallen into ownership of a Vaxstation 2000,
:RD54, TK50, monochrome monitor. I would like to load VMS 5.x from the TK50
:by using standalone BACKUP. I find myself documentally challenged, however.
:Anyone remember the magic BACKUP command line? Something like
:
:$ BACKUP MUA0: DUA0: 
:
:I suppose, but maybe with command line switches or something. Using this
:command line generates:
:
:%BACKUP-F-NOVOLDATA, volume summary data for DUA0: not found - /IMAGE cannot
:be used.

  From within standalone BACKUP, use:

	BACKUP/IMAGE MUA0:VMS05x.B/SAVE DUA0:/INITIALIZE

  This assumes you really want to clobber DUA0:, and it assumes you
  have a distribution kit with a version of the operating system able
  to boot the VAXstation 2000 system, and it assumes the tape is
  positioned at apoint in front of the VMS05x.B saveset on the tape.
  If you are not sure of the version or of the saveset name, you
  can use

	BACKUP MUA0:/SAVE/LIST

  Repeatedly, once for each saveset.  This is rather tedious, but
  if you don't know what's on the tape and if you don't have a
  system you can look at this tape, it may be the best option.

  You can use /REWIND on the tape specification parameter on a BACKUP
  command -- this will rewind the tape, and it will start the search
  for the specified saveset from the beginning of the tape.

