[ I may be slightly biased - sue me ;]

In article <chris.morgan-2012951759030001@baesema4.demon.co.uk>,
Chris Morgan <chris.morgan@baesema.co.uk> wrote:
>
>  How good is Linux on Alpha?

The linux kernel itself looks stable: I've been using it on my home
machine now for a few months as my primary system, and have yet to see
any problems.  If you know linux on x86, the alpha port is very
familiar, and is based on exactly the same sources that the x86 port is
done from. 

However, the user-level setup is not as smooth as linux/x86, although
the RedHat CD helped a lot.  Notably, Linux/alpha doesn't have shared
libraries yet.  This is not a limitation of the kernel - in fact the
OSF/1 shared libraries work fine - but it's brought about mainly by the
fact that there doesn't seem to be any ELF standard for the alpha.  Most
linux developers feel that the old a.out-based shared libraries are not
worth pursuing, so we either have to come up with a standard of our own
(and be incompatible with Digital UNIX whenever DEC goes ELF) or take a
wait-and-see approach. 

Also, Linux/x86 has commercial programs, which haven't appeared for
Linux/alpha (like DOOM, Mathematica etc).  Of course, Linux/alpha might
use Digital unix binaries instead. 

>  What kind of machines does it run on? (e.g. are they the machines that
>would otherwise run Digital Unix, or will the "NT dream machines" do Linux
>as well?)

It runs on the PCI-based NoName (166 and 233 MHz 21066) and Cabriolet
(275MHz 21064A), as well as the EISA-based older Jensen hardware.  It
also runs on the Multias, I'm told, although I haven't seen that
personally. 

The Multia and the Jensen don't have any X server available for them,
though. 

>  How popular is Alpha/Linux?

Hard to tell.  There are certainly people out there using it, but I
can't give any numbers. 

>  Did DEC help out with the port is it grassroots stuff like the rest of Linux?

They've helped out with hardware and there is also a linux team inside
DEC.  I don't know how official the linux team is (and it's smaller than
their NT team, I do believe ;-), but they have certainly helped out a
lot. 

>  What statements have DEC made? Can we expect affordable Alpha based
>machines that run Linux and offer high-performance, or will Intel dominate
>the low-end/home market from now on?

I don't think DEC has made any official statements, and I can't answer
for them.  I certainly like my alpha (I have the Cabriolet at home), and
I hope linux/alpha will work out.  I abhor the thought that we'd have
NT-only alpha's out there.. 

		Linus



