<Here's some memories from another net poster:>

With regard to tape drives, here is an experience on the IBM 7094 at
U of Chicago in 1968 (much later than the reference in the original note).

Someone discovered that you could get tape drives to play notes by issuing
a read command and a blast channel in a loop of appropriate frequency so
that the head rattling backwards and forward emitted a hum. This was much
more interesting than the traditional method of playing music (timed loops
generating RF from the core unit that could be picked up on a radio), because
there were multiple drives/channels, and so polyphony was possible.

The CE came in one day to find the machine playing a Bach fugue, and he
went absolutely berserk, informing us in no uncertain terms that this program
would destroy the tape drives in short order.

Actually the phenomenon of hardware systems that can be physically destroyed
by bad programs is common these days (ranging from the high performance
planes that require constant corrections to lowly PC monitors which can be
destroyed easily by sending inappropriate bandwidth signals to them).


<and another unattributed reference:>

   Gee, we used to get polyphony out of a PDP-1 using RF around the
same time.  The trick was that the amount of noise generated was a
function of the number of one bits in the word.  I wish I still had a
tape of the nightly sign-off for WTBS.  (Yes, MIT later traded the
call to Ted Turner for some new equipment...)  It was a version of the
Star-Spangled Banner arranged as a fugue.

-- 
