From: mogul@pa.dec.com (Jeffrey Mogul)
Subject: a little more about -xtaso and -taso in Digital UNIX
Date: 1995/12/16
Message-ID: <4at731$17e@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 121941112
references: <4angp1$bp5@topaz.sensor.com> <qxpn38wgwkx.fsf@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> <danpop.818990982@rscernix>
organization: DEC Western Research
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <danpop.818990982@rscernix> danpop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>Second, the way -xtaso works.  Your pointers are still 64-bit, but the
>most significant 32 bits are zero.  This can be easily proved:
>
>    ues5:~/tmp 76> cat test.c
>    #include <stdio.h>
>    main(int argc, char **argv)
>    {
>	char *p;
>	printf("%ld\n", (long)sizeof p);
>	return 0;
>    }
>    ues5:~/tmp 77> cc -xtaso test.c
>    ues5:~/tmp 78> ./a.out
>    8

Actually, this is still a little confused.

There are two different kinds of flags:
	-taso	tells the *linker* to load the program in a 32-bit
		address space (i.e., all program addresses have
		the upper 32 bits zero).
	-xtaso	tells the *compiler* to pay attention to the
		#pragma pointer_size directives

Here's another example program (dan.c):
    #include <stdio.h>
    main(int argc, char **argv)
    {
    char *p = "Hello WRL";
    printf("%ld %p\n", (long)sizeof p, p);
    return 0;
    }
and here is the result of compiling it several ways.    
    % cc dan.c 
    % a.out
    8 140000010
    % cc -xtaso dan.c
    % a.out
    8 140000010

Note: -xtaso has NO effect on the most significant bits of the pointer.
That address has 9 hex digits

    % cc -taso dan.c
    % a.out
    8 14000010

Aha.  Now we have only 8 hex digits in the address.

Obviously, if you are compiling a module with -xtaso (and using
#pragma pointer_size (short) anywhere) you must link the program
using -taso, or the results will be unpleasant.

>The easiest way to get 32-bit pointers is to use -xtaso_short instead:

A little too easy.  I would advise never using -xtaso_short, since
unless you are not #including any header files, it's likely to cause
trouble.  Use -xtaso and #pragma pointer_size.

-Jeff



