I would concur with Robert Garcia's excellent analysis.  The basic
consideration is that your 512 megs of RAM does not push you into
the range of the larger Alpha 8x00 boxes.  Providing that 4 CPUs
is not to limiting, the 2100 series could be quite cost-effective.

Depending on how you structure your application, expansion might
take place with an additional box rather than a bigger box.  VMS
has had clusters for years (with features far outstriping Unix
"clusters" from any vendors), while DEC Unix will apparently precede
VMS in support for "memory channel" interconnects between multiple
boxes.  The trick with memory channel would seem to be one of using
the fast hardware connectivity without coupling the OS software (or
application software) so tightly that the whole thing crashes together.

I mention crash resistance because I am a VMS bigot, but certainly
there are lots of applications where crash resistance is not of
primary importance.

