In article <4asmqf$vf2@taco.cc.ncsu.edu>,
Nehal Naishadh Desai <nndesai2@eos.ncsu.edu> wrote:
>does anyone know whether the PAL (private architecture library) for the
>alphas  come standard with the machine.  Also any ideas on how to
>implement the PAL with alpha assembly code.

Of course you get PAL with any OS you buy from Digital.

If you're into writing your own, I would strongly recommend buying a
development board from Digital: they come with sources of something
OSF/1-like, a modified Alpha (GNU) gas and a program called pvc
(PALcode violation checker).

Believe me, you don't want to be without pvc: in the worst case you
can damage the chip with duff PALcode, but even if you don't go that
far, you can definitely find yourself with totally incomprehensible
halts ... once you reboot, you've typically lost everything.  pvc is
designed to find those silly "you can't write this register 2 cycles
after you wrote that one" errors that the chip reference manuals list.
