X-Deja-AN: 122206779
distribution: world
references: <4anho1$6jg@cloner2.ix.netcom.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corp
reply-to: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <4anho1$6jg@cloner2.ix.netcom.com>, <> writes:
:I cannot find an NFS client for Windows 95 anywhere.  Does
:ANYBODY know where I can find one?

   You will want to ask Microsoft, or ask in one of the newsgroups
   that more commonly discusses Microsoft Windows 95 topics.

:I'm trying to connect an DEC Alpha 3000 to our network, which
:runs mostly on Windows 95.

   The DEC 3000 series systems run either OpenVMS or Digital UNIX.
   By "...our network, which runs mostly on Windows 95", I assume
   you mean you have a pile of Windows 95 systems connected to a
   network of some ilk -- you'll need to indicate what protocols
   are in use on the network.

:                              The Alpha is running NFS, so I am
:assuming this is what I need to connect.  Is this right?

   Assuming this is an Ethernet or IEEE 802.3 network, you can run
   most any protocol over the Ethernet/802.3 network without causing
   any problems for any other protocol(s) that might be running over
   the same Ethernet/802.3 network.

   There are two hunks to NFS: the client, and the server -- the
   server end is the end nearer to the physical storage, while the
   client is usually nearer to where the user or the application
   is running.  If you want to "serve" storage from the Windows 95
   system(s), you need an NFS server package.  If you want to access
   NFS disks from the Windows 95 system (or any other operating system
   for that matter), you need a "client" package for the system.

   Various operating systems and various NFS packages have both the
   "client" and the "server", while some don't.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
