Dirk Grunwald (grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu) wrote:


: [foobar-33] cc -xtaso -o foo foo.c
: [foobar-34] ./foo
: woof = 0x140000d0
: block = 0x11ffedd8
: woof = woof
: block = ./foo


: Those look like 32-bit pointer values to me.
: What am I missing?

You are missing the -xtaso_short flag.  If you'd have
run "sizeof woof" there it would still be 8.  All -xtaso
does by itself is force the vitrual addresses down to
below 4 gig.  The pointers are still 64 bits long.

-Ron



