In <4aqnhp$dpn@usenet.pa.dec.com> mogul@pa.dec.com (Jeffrey Mogul) writes:

>Here's a correct example of mixing 32 and 64 bit pointers:

It is, however, a bad example of using fprintf :-)

>    #include <stdio.h>
>    main()
>    {
>	    char *format1 = "sizeof(format1) = %d\n";
>    #pragma pointer_size (short)
>	    char *format2 = "sizeof(format2) = %d\n";
>    
>	    fprintf(stdout, format1, sizeof(format1));
>	    fprintf(stdout, format2, sizeof(format2));
>    }

%d expects an int.  sizeof returns a size_t value, which on OSF/1 is an
unsigned long.  The result is undefined behaviour.  There are two 
independent reasons which make the code to work as intended on DEC OSF/1
(32-bit int's are promoted to 64 bits when they are passed as function
arguments and OSF/1 is little endian, anyway).  But this is not enough
to make it correct code.

Dan
