Date: 1995/12/07
Message-ID: <1995Dec7.185029.30899@ucl.ac.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 121372681
sender: news@ucl.ac.uk (Usenet News System)
organization: Dept. of Physics & Astronomy, UCL
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Hi,

I currently have a DEC LJ250 inkjet printer connected to a DECserver 200 with
the queue sys_inkjet spooling jobs to it from an Alpha 2000/300 running DU 3.2.
It prints Postscript using a Ghostview filter to convert to HP-PCL format. The
printcap entry is as follows:

sys_inkjet|lp5|5:\
        :af=/var/adm/lpacct:\
        :br#9600:\
        :ct=LAT:\
        :fc#0177777:fs#03:\
        :ts=ZUDS6:\
        :op=PORT_4:\
        :rw:\
        :lf=/var/adm/lpd-err:\
        :lp=/dev/tty0s:\
        :mx#0:\
        :if=/usr/local/bin/ps2lj250:\
        :os=inkjet:\
        :sh:\
        :xc#0177777:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:\
        :xs#044000:\
        :sd=/var/spool/lpd/sys_inkjet:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:

The input filter, /usr/local/bin/ps2lj250, contains

        GS=/usr/local/bin/gs
        cat - | \
        ( $GS -q -dNOPAUSE -sDEVICE=declj250 -r180x180 -sOutputFile=- - )

and everything works fine. Recently I've acquired a second LJ250 which I want
to set up in a similar way. Now, if I copy the printcap entry, set up a queue
sys_inkjet2, create the tty for it and use latcp to "bind" the tty to another
DECserver 200 port (which has the same setup as the first) e.g.

        # /usr/sbin/latcp -A -p tty0w -H ZUDS6 -R PORT_8

everything works fine again. The problem is that I want connect the
printer to a DECserver 300 and if I repeat the process as above the printer
just outputs garbage, a few lines per page and if left alone will happily
work its way through a whole box of paper.

I've connected a terminal to the DS300 port and that works OK, I've set up a
queue with an old LA75 dot-matrix printer on the same port and that prints text
files OK; it's only with the LJ250 that the problem occurs.

Any suggestions as to what's going on/what to do will be much appreciated.


John Deacon
UCL Starlink Manager (acting...)




