kth@srv.net (Kevin Handy) writes:

|In article <4a25vd$b1o@trog.dra.hmg.gb>,
|	ceilbeck@dra.hmg.gb (Chris Eilbeck) says:
|>Is there a free unix available for a Vaxstation 3100?  Seems a waste to have
|>a machine doing nothing just because I can't stand VMS.
|
|Currently, there isn't, but there is a group working on FreeBSD for
|the MicroVax II, and the 780.
|See http://www.netbsd.com/ for more information.

More specifically, NetBSD runs on the MicroVAX II and the VAX-11/750.
NetBSD is not FreeBSD; the two are different projects, and while the
FreeBSD group has focused more on maximizing performance on the Intel
platform, the NetBSD team has emphasized cross-platform portability.
There's communication and code sharing between the groups, of course.

NetBSD currently runs on Amiga, Atari, DEC Alpha/MIPS/VAX, HP9000/300,
Intel i[345]86, Macintosh m68k, Motorola m68k, PC532, Sun SPARC and
Sun 3 systems, with more ports in the works.  It runs binaries from
the "original" operating systems on most of these -- for the ones from
DEC, that means binary compatibility with OSF/1, Ultrix and 4.3-Reno
on the Alpha, MIPS and VAX platforms, respectively.

Oh, and it's http://www.netbsd.org/ -- "org", not "com".  It is free,
after all!  :-)

-tih



