The 'no-name' 21066 or 21066A boards seem to be good only for software 
development anyway. Alpha XL 266 is cheap enough to compete with high-end 
Pentiums at 133 MHz and it costs similarly for a 32 MB NT configuration.

On XL 266, Real3D Windows NT raytracing is on average 2.5 to 3 times 
faster than on equally configured 133 MHz Pentium with burst cache! If 
you happen to use some of those 333 MHz 21164 mainboards Aspen is 
starting to sell next week, you'll get further 2.5 times speedup... An 
this mainboard in a good 4 MB cache config shouldn't cost much.

Re: Pentium Pro - you'll probably see several inexpensive Dual 400 MHz 
21164A systems in the next six months from several vendors. These will 
outperford Dual Pentium Pro-200 systems between two and five times in 
Windows NT (forget BAPCO based on old-fashioned Word, Excel, PowerPoint 
and other Intel-emulated code) apps.

