>
>The problem we are facing is that TP-PMD on GIGAswitch are dying. This
>is indicated by the left LED on the TP-PMD going off. 
>

    Note that the left status LED on the PMD indicates the PHY status when you
are running the BL2.2 level firmware (which is what the FGL-4 revision level
indicates).  Off means "ready to connect", not a PMD failure.  Your FGL-2
firmware is older which would have different LED behaviour.  The BL2.2 release
notes document the changed behaviour.

>One PMD was dead on arrival. Another two worked for some time and then
>went dead completely. Two more were working for some days and then
>started giving Link Confidence Test (LCT) error (the left LED was steady
>Amber). It toggled between LCT error state and working state for few
>hours before becoming totally dead.

    I strongly recommend that you immediately upgrade all your cards to the
same firmware baselevel.  From your post, you are mixing BL2.1 and BL2.2
revisions.  Note that the behaviour of the PMD LEDs change with the BL2.2
release as the GIGAswitch/FDDI product was changed to conform to the new
behavour of our DEChub product line.

>Another problem that we have observed is that FGL-4 card some times, on
>its own, power cycles. When this happen the hosts connected to it
>observe the failed n/w and programs using the FDDI connection hangs.
>This problem happens in a totally random manner. Sometimes the card will
>power cycle 2-3 times in 15mins and then will work stably for 2 days
>before power cycling again.

    Most likely the FGL-4 card has error log entries which indicate what the
source of the problem is.  Once again - it never a good idea to mix firmware
revisions - I will contact you via mail to get a copy of your error log for
diagnosis.

>1 TP-PMD was dead on arrival. Next one died after operating for nearly 12
>days. The one after that was the first PMD to give LCT error. It died
>2 days after the previous casuality. This was also the time when we 
>observed repeated power cycling of the working FGL-4 card. The next 
>two PMD died after 8 more days. This takes the total no. of bad PMDs to 5
>out of 8. That's pretty high failure rate.

    It is clearly wrong to be getting cards which don't work on arrival.  I
will communicate your problems to the appropriate people here in our group.

>The biggest anxiety we have is that PMDs which were working, fail
>after some time. At least from what we have observed the problem doesn't
>seem to be with PCI-FDDI card or the UTP cabling. One discrepancy we
>have is that some of the cards in GIGAswitch/FDDI don't have the latest
>firmware. The firmware revision status is as follows.

   ...again, it would be most helpful if I could examine the error logs and
SNMP counters for the failed ports.  I know of no endemic problems with our
TP-PMDs, but I'll do some further research..

>Any idea whether old firmware revision can be a cause of the observed
>problems?

    I don't believe it should cause any problems, but there have been several
bug fixes and improvements made since the firmware you are running.  Again, I
strongly recommend that you upgrade as soon as possible.

    GIGAswitch/FDDI firmware is available via anonymous FTP on 
gatekeeper.dec.com in the /pub/Digital/Gigaswitch directory.  Copy and reade
the readme.txt file for a list of the appropriate files.

    It is also available off the Network Product Business home page,
http://www.networks.digital.com/

    You can go directly to the GIGAswitch/FDDI page at:
http://www.networks.digital.com/html/gigaswitch-fddi.html

