"Thumper!" <thumper@labiker.org> writes:

>I understand the advantages of going from a 16-bit OS to a 32 bit.  16 bit 
>obviously limited many things like memory addressing, etc, to unrealistic (in 
>today's world) limits that we could do better than.
>
>Now, how many things really USE 64 bit functions?  Or is this much more of a vanity 
>thing than anything else?

It's not as much a matter of 64-bit operations as it is a matter of being
able to transparently address more than 4 GB of real and/or virtual memory.
If your application needs 6 GB of RAM to provide decent performance, you
cannot use it on a 32-bit system.  Right now there aren't many such 
applications, and only the high end (read over $100k) Alpha systems can
support this amount of RAM (which, BTW, costs many $100k's), so most
people buying Alpha systems don't make any use of the 64-bitness of the
architecture (unless they do a lot of 64-bit integer arithmetic), but
for large database servers, 64-bit systems cannot be beatten.  The main
selling point of the Alpha-based systems is their speed, not their
64-bit architecture.

Note that other vendors have started to produce 64-bit systems, based on
their own architectures.

If you're considering NT, forget the 64-bit issue.  NT is a 32-bit OS and
is going to stay like this for a while.  And the fastest NT boxes (as far
as running 3rd party applications is concerned) are the new Pentium Pro
based systems.  NT on Alpha is a waste of money and computing resources.

Dan
