One of the research topics that's receiving lots of attention from the
likes of my group is Single Address Space OSes (SASOS).  These show
much promise of techniques for squeezing a bit more out of the
processor, which is rather important when you're running high speed
networks/high-performance multimedia apps.  (Our user-level network
drivers perform better than `traditional' kernel-integrated Un*x ones,
for example.)

Believe me, a SASOS on a <=32-bit VA space is not a little limited.  I
had hoped that the 21164 was going to increase the rather feeble 43
bits of VA that the 21064 gives you, but those 11 extra bits do make
the difference between hope and reality.
