>In article <1995Dec8.184904.6425@cmkrnl> jeh@cmkrnl.com (Jamie
>Hanrahan, Kernel Mode Systems) writes:
>
>   > I'm looking for pointers on Alpha AXP memory management, escpecially
>   > about the protection of bits of memory. I'm looking all over, but the
>   > library doesn't have anything. Webcrawler and others are coming up
>   > empty too... :(
>
>   Under which operating system?  They're different...
>
>That's the trouble; I have to port a highly experimental kernel with
>no mem management whatsoever to an alpha AXP. Am I totally free to do
>my own mgmt or can the alpha lend me a hand (Watching for memory
>overruns comes to mind).

You're pretty much on your own.  Read the architecture manual and the
chip reference manual.  Read the PALcode for whatever OSs you can get
your hands on (I think the Huygens group has OSF/1 PALcode, but even
if not, DEC are fairly free with it nowadays).  Read any OS sources
you can lay your hands on (again, I think the Huygens group has an
OSF/1 source licence).

Breathe deeply, construct an architecture, and go for it.  The chips
themselves don't offer much more than basic building blocks -- the
architecture isn't like the VAX, where all the TLB filling and so on
happened automagically, so you're into writing PALcode as like as not.
