Thumper! (thumper@labiker.org) wrote:

> I understand the advantages of going from a 16-bit OS to a 32 bit.  16 bit 
> obviously limited many things like memory addressing, etc, to unrealistic (in 
> today's world) limits that we could do better than.

> Now, how many things really USE 64 bit functions?  Or is this much more of a vanity 
> thing than anything else?

Going from a 32-bit OS to a 64-bit OS gives you (more or less) the same jump
that going from a 16-bit OS to a 32-bit OS does -- that is, larger flat
memory spaces, support for larger disks and so forth.  

You might dismiss this as "just vanity", I suppose, but there are classes
of real world applications for which addressabile disk space in the
10's of terabytes is desired and for whom real (physical) memory in the
gigabyte range is a real win.  These aren't research or design goals -- they
are real, commercially needed (and viable) applications that run every
day.
