mbrodeur@homer.zko.dec.com (Michael Brodeur) writes:

>Thumper! (thumper@labiker.org) wrote:
>
>> I understand the advantages of going from a 16-bit OS to a 32 bit.  16 bit 
>> obviously limited many things like memory addressing, etc, to unrealistic (in 
>> today's world) limits that we could do better than.
>
>> Now, how many things really USE 64 bit functions?  Or is this much more of a vanity 
>> thing than anything else?
>
>Going from a 32-bit OS to a 64-bit OS gives you (more or less) the same jump
>that going from a 16-bit OS to a 32-bit OS does -- that is, larger flat
>memory spaces, support for larger disks and so forth.  

Except that the difference between 64k of address space and 4 GB is much
more significant than the difference between 4 GB and 2^64 bytes, which,
for most people is purely academic today (how many GB of RAM can you
install in an AlphaStation?).

>You might dismiss this as "just vanity", I suppose, but there are classes
>of real world applications for which addressabile disk space in the
>10's of terabytes is desired and for whom real (physical) memory in the
>gigabyte range is a real win.  These aren't research or design goals -- they
>are real, commercially needed (and viable) applications that run every
>day.

Good.  Now tell us how many percents of the installed DEC OSF/1 base is
running on hardware suitable for these applications.  I guess it's way
under 1%, all the other systems having less than 4 GB of RAM and being
configured with less than 4 GB of swap space.  The rest of the 99+% systems
pay the penalty of 64-bit pointers without getting any advantage.

A 32-bit version of DEC OSF/1 would have made a lot of sense, for all those
who don't need more and whose vanity doesn't require a 64-bit OS.  The
large Oracle servers and other huge systems could use the 64-bit flavour,
while Joe User would be happily using the 32-bit one on his AlphaStation,
without the performance hit and waste of memory caused by the need to
manipulate humongously sized pointers.

Dan
