In article <4aic03$3u1@cantua.canterbury.ac.nz>, E.Havelaar@Regy.Canterbury.Ac.NZ (Ed Havelaar) writes:
:We have an RdB database on top of VAX VMS (soon to migrate to Alpha) for which 
:I need to generate a unique account number whenever I create a new record.  I 
:wonder if anyone has any experience on what the best way to do this is.
:Options that have crossed my mind are:

  You don't mention the frequency of this particular operation.

  I use imbedded SQL; I read the "max id" value from the database, and
  and write the (incremented) result back to the database.

    EXEC SQL
    SET TRANSACTION READ WRITE WAIT ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE RESERVING
        xxx_ID_TABLE FOR PROTECTED WRITE;
    if (SQLCA.SQLCODE != 0) SQL$SIGNAL();

  There are various suggestions and tradeoffs on this particular topic
  discussed the Rdb manuals.

	--

  If you want to use a faster mechanism, read the ID value into a lock
  value block and maintain it there -- this does not help you from a PC,
  but it does work nicely across all nodes in a VMScluster.  You'll need
  to design the restart code to reload the value in the lock value block
  from the highest known ID value present in the database -- this logic
  is needed for the recovery after a system crash.

	--

  I don't recommend pursuing the RMS file services approach, nor the
  volatile memory mechanism, mentioned in your original posting.  Both
  can be made to work, but they do not appear to have advantages over
  storage in Rdb or storage in a lock value block.

