In article <1995Dec13.034912.11596@cs.rochester.edu>,
Leonidas Kontothanassis <kthanasi@cs.rochester.edu> wrote:
>Does anyone know how the DEC-Alpha first level cache is organized,
>I want to know if it is physically or virtually indexed and if it
>is physically or virtually tagged.

It depends on the version of the alpha chip.  EV4 (21064) uses a 8kB
direct-mapped physically tagged first-level cache (8kB is also the
page-size, so for indexing purposes it doesn't matter if we use the
virtual or physical address), while the cache in the EV45 (21064A) is
16kB virtually indexed and physically tagged. 

(From my understanding, the EV45 doubled the cache-size by essentially
doing a 2-bank 8kB cache, where the bank is selected by the virtual
address (bit 13), and then within the bank we have more-or-less the same
organization as in the EV4). 

The EV5 seems to have the same 8kB primary data cache as the EV4 (but
it's faster - 2 vs 3 cycle latency, I think).  The EV5 icache is
different, though: it seems to be virtually tagged.  And the EV5 also
has the added 96kB 3-way unified secondary cache, of course. 

		Linus



