I've had a fair number of responses to my request below, and since not many of 
them seem to have turned up in the newsgroups, I thought I'd post a quick 
summary (and another question).

First my question:

Suppose I have a program that takes ages to run and keeps a read-only 
transaction open for its duration (e.g. to perform calculations on all 100000 
records in a table),  Every now and again, I want to assign an account number.  
This involves starting a read-write transaction for as short a time as 
possible.  How can I do this without terminating my hours-long 
read-only transaction and somehow figuring out where and how to recommence it?

Now my original question, and a bit of a summary.

>
>We have an RdB database on top of VAX VMS (soon to migrate to Alpha) for 
which 
>I need to generate a unique account number whenever I create a new record.  I 
>wonder if anyone has any experience on what the best way to do this is.

The general consensus seems to be:
(1) Triggers would work, sort of.  This doesn't really tell me what code to 
put in the trigger though -- I understand triggers are just a way of 
automatically executing some code on (say) an insert operation, so it would be 
handy for ODBC clients.  I got the impression from a number of people that 
triggers are not overly fast, and we're looking at a peak load of about 400 
inserts per hour, so I doubt this is the way to go.

(2) Getting Max(Acc_No)+1 from a table is too slow.

(3) Better to store the next available number in a table of its own, and have 
each application lock and increment it when it needs a new one.  A few 
suggested the "Jerry Leichter" approach of splitting the available numbers 
into blocks with one record governing assignments from each block, and then 
using some random number to figure out which record to lock.  We probably 
don't have enough load to justify bothering with the added complexity.

(4) Nobody seemed to think there was any great advantage in using RMS files.



