rdd@access4.digex.net (R. D. Davis) writes:

>5) Music 11 at MIT
>------------------

I have no idea what part it played (pun intended) in the development
of later music programs, but back in the early 60s Pete Sampson wrote
a music compiler for the PDP-1 at MIT.  The entire system had 4K words
(18 bits) with a 5 usec cycle, and the only "storage" device was the
paper tape punch (no magnetic disks!).  Input to the program was a
paper tape (prepared on a Flexowriter); the output was implemented
by toggling three of the sense lights (lamps on the console that could
be turned on and off under program control); the wires to these lamps
were taken out of the machine, run through a simple homebrew mixer, and
fed to a Heathkit audio amplifier.  Each of the sense lights was used
to output a different part.

The quality of the output was poor by today's standards, but it wasn't
at all bad, especially given that the PDP-1 cost a fraction of the price
of the big mainframes of that era, and (based on recordings that I've got
of music from them) could generate music of equivalent quality.

Joe Morris / MITRE



