chefren (chefren@dds.nl) wrote:

: I have no special connection to Exabyte but we service lots machines 
: with 8200 in the field without problems. We DO had problems with 
: developping software for them since they do strange things with SCSI.

: If you have software that can handle the 8200 from the SCSI side it
: will work for you, years and years.

: (Always interested in other meanings, feel free to comment.)

We have a stack of broken 8200's that say differently.  The reliability
figures tend to vary widely from site to site.  It's not a SCSI problem
as we can just substitute a fixed or new drive and it works fine.  Actually,
the thing we've primarily noticed sticking 8200's into places where disk
drives go is that they suck too much power when the drive goes through
it's bumping and grinding to load the tape (or when it's just resetting
itself even when there is no tape installed).  This we've found will
pull the power supply out of spec on PS/2's causing them to crash
(was particularly annoying as the drive waits a minute before commencing
it's bumping and grinding ritual so you can actually start working before
it blows out on you).

Our guess as to the reliability problem is that they tend to degrade if
not used regularlly.  We infrequently use ours and get high mortality
rates on both 8200's and 8500's.

-Ron



