Hello,

We have a DEC GIGAswitch/FDDI. Recently we installed a FGL-4 (i.e. 4
port SAS FDDI card) with TP-PMDs. These TP-PMD ports are connected via
Cat-5 UTP cable to DEC PCI-FDDI card, with TP-PMD, sitting on Alpha
workstations.

The problem we are facing is that TP-PMD on GIGAswitch are dying. This
is indicated by the left LED on the TP-PMD going off. 

One PMD was dead on arrival. Another two worked for some time and then
went dead completely. Two more were working for some days and then
started giving Link Confidence Test (LCT) error (the left LED was steady
Amber). It toggled between LCT error state and working state for few
hours before becoming totally dead.

In all these cases there was no failure on the PCI-FDDI card with
TP-PMD.

Is there any one else out there using a GIGAswitch/FDDI with TP-PMD
ports who can comment on these observations. I would like to know your
experiences with the product
- uptime
- problems faced
- specific care taken for cabling

Another problem that we have observed is that FGL-4 card some times, on
its own, power cycles. When this happen the hosts connected to it
observe the failed n/w and programs using the FDDI connection hangs.
This problem happens in a totally random manner. Sometimes the card will
power cycle 2-3 times in 15mins and then will work stably for 2 days
before power cycling again.

I would like to add that we have been using two FGL-2 cards with MMF PMD
for last 10 months without any such problems.

Just to complete the picture we had ordered two FGL-4 cards with 4
TP-PMDs each. Out of the one FGL-4 card was dead on arrival.

1 TP-PMD was dead on arrival. Next one died after operating for nearly 12
days. The one after that was the first PMD to give LCT error. It died
2 days after the previous casuality. This was also the time when we 
observed repeated power cycling of the working FGL-4 card. The next 
two PMD died after 8 more days. This takes the total no. of bad PMDs to 5
out of 8. That's pretty high failure rate.

Local DEC support people are replacing the faulty cards and PMDs, but in
India that process normally take 4-6 weeks as no spares are locally
available.

The biggest anxiety we have is that PMDs which were working, fail
after some time. At least from what we have observed the problem doesn't
seem to be with PCI-FDDI card or the UTP cabling. One discrepancy we
have is that some of the cards in GIGAswitch/FDDI don't have the latest
firmware. The firmware revision status is as follows.

Card                                     Required     Current
					revision     revision
SCP Operation                            2.2          2.1
SCP Bootblock (BB)                       2.0          2.0
SCP Downline Load (DL)                   1.0          1.0
FGL-2                                    2.2          2.0
FGL-4                                    2.0          2.0
CLK                                      2.1          2.1
PSC                                      2.0          2.0

Any idea whether old firmware revision can be a cause of the observed
problems?

Please reply by email as our news feed is not very reliable.

Thanks in advance,

