medcm@uno.edu wrote:
>I am currently writing a program in MACRO-32 to run on a VAX system.  When
>I use the instruction EDIV R5,R0,R0,R1 , according to the technical specs,
>the computer should take the value R1:R0, a quadword composed of the values
>in R1 and R0, with R1 more significant, and divide it by the doubleword
>value in R5, then store the doubleword answer in R0 and the doubleword
>remainder in R1.  However, when I execute that instruction, with R0=0, 
>R1=1, and R5=2, instead of returning R1=0 and R0=^x80000000, as it
>seemingly should, it returns R1=0 and R0=0.  Is there any explanation for
>this, and are the technical specs that I read correct?
>
> 
>        Michael DeSalvo
>        medcm@jazz.ucc.uno.edu
> 
>

I got curious about your question and decided to try it with the 
debugger.  The VAX is behaving as advertised.  The values that you are 
using are causing an integer overflow (examine the PSL).  

Remember that the result of the division is a longword (not an unsigned
long).  The value that you were expecting to be stored into R0 has it's 
high bit set indicating that the number is negative.  Anytime the result 
of an operation should be a positive number and the result would be 
negative if stored in the destination, an overflow occurs.  

Try your program again using a larger divisor an you will get your 
expected results.

