I've always enjoyed using benchmarks to test and tweak out my system, but 
benchmarks really need to be taken with a grain of salt.  What is being tested 
with this test?  I'm guessing (because I don't know) that integer performance 
and 2d graphics performance (ie scolling speed in word?) is what is being 
tested.  Spec marks show that integer performance on a Pentium Pro 200Mhz is 
better than an Alpha 21164 at 300Mhz.  Well.. I didn't buy my Alpha for it's 
integer performance.. and Word/Excel have run fast enough for me since the 
introduction of the 486.  I got an Alpha for it's floating point peformance.. 
and while the Pentium Pro has apparently made great advances in integer 
performance.. it lags the Alpha.. by quite a bit still in floating point 
performance.

Ansys (Finite Element Analysis code.. check www.ansys.com) has a standard set 
of problems they use to benchmark different systems and operating systems.  
Below I have copied a small portion of the latest listings and as you'll see.. 
Intel has nothing on DEC.. for real Engineering Apps.  How does a 5598 second 
run on a P133 w/128 megs of Ram look compared to 502 seconds on a similarly 
configured DEC 21164 266Mhz running the same OS.  By my count, that's more 
than 11 times faster.  Now compare that with the 300Mhz.. and the 333Mhz.  
These processors are out in quantity now.  By the time that Intel gets out the 
P6 200Mhz in quantity, DEC will have the newer .35 micron 21164 .. with 
performance increases near 50% over current .5 micron chips with the same 
clock speed (http://www.windowsnt.digital.com/press/951114al.htm).  All this, 
not to mention rumors of a 400+ Mhz chip out sometime next year.  For raw 
floating point power.. your not going to beat a DEC.. not this year anyway 
<grin>.  Granted I haven't seen these tests run on a Pentium Pro.. but Spec 
marks show that floating point performance on a Pentium Pro is approximately 
2X that of a P133.  I'll be anxious to see the results when they come out.




