kjh6@psu.edu (Ken Hoover) wrote:

>  I want to upgrade our AlpahServer 2000 to Windows NT Server v3.51, but 
>the release notes for the upgrade state that if you have a SWXCR RAID 
>controller (as I do), then you must upgrade the RAID firmware to 
>version 3.11 before upgrading... however, I can't find the firmware 
>anywhere.  Can some kind soul please tell me where/how to get this update 
>so we can move on?

Peter Katz <70724.1447@compuserve.com> wrote:
> The SWXCR (PCI Raid controller aka KZPSC-??) comes with firmware on it 
> and I don't belive it has changed any over the past year - it should 
> be v2.19 and I don't think you want to be messing with it.

I concur that you probably don't want to mess with the SWXCR firmware.  Also,
note that Mr. Hoover might have either the EISA SWXCR (aka KZESC) or the
PCI SWXCR (aka KZPSC).  They take different firmware versions; don't attempt
to load one onto the other!

Mr. Katz continues:
>I think you're looking for the RAID STANDALONE CONFIGURATION UTILITY V 3.11 
> aka (I think) as SWXCRMGR.
>
>You can pick up the latest of this from DIGITALS FTP site; I've seen it out there.
>
>Before you start out on this project, you want to make sure that you have the ECU 
>diskette v1.8 and be sure you read all the notes very carefully. Try to find 
>something that says "ALPHASERVER 2000/2100 Read this first", etc.

Concur.  A couple of other suggestions: 

    - The "Customer Update" article on NT V3.51 (found walking down from 
      www.digital.com, or directly by:

            http://www.digital.com/info/Customer-Update/950815006.txt.html

      says that the kit (if ordered from Digital) contains the updated ECU.

    - The ECU ".cfg" files may be found via the gatekeeper.dec.com ftp area
      under "private/fwaxp".  Or point your browser directly at

           ftp://ftp.digital.com/private/fwaxp/readme.txt
       
      As mentioned in another thread, the ECU executable itself is not there (it's
      separately licensed, not originally a Digital product); but Digital's cfg
      files are there. It may be easier to call 1-800-DIGITAL and order 
      QA-01YAA-HC, since it looks like the executable itself may have recently
      changed.

    - Unfortunately, I couldn't find the SWXCRMGR utility just now looking
      around the ftp archives on gatekeeper.dec.com.  From www.digital.com,
      I found:

           http://www.windowsnt.digital.com/support/drivers/drivers.htm

      which contains the text:

           Configuration Utilities
                 Latest EISA Configuration Files for All EISA Platforms 
                     (not yet available) 
                 Latest RAID Configuration Files for the Mylex RAID 
                      Configuration Utility (not yet available) 

      I'm surprised to see that, as I thought they were available by now.
      Perhaps this is another instance of licensing limits (Mylex is the
      original author of the utilities); feel free to encourage your Digital
      contacts to encourage the lawyers to get the utility on-line.

    - In general, you may want to get hooked into telephone support for this 
      project!  The SWXCR config procedures are easy once you've 
      done them a few times; but when you're learning them it can be
      EXTREMELY helpful to have good advice as you go.

If you have all the pre-requisites in hand, this is NOT a hard upgrade.
I did a painless upgrade of both a PCI-based Alpha and a PCI-based Pentium
from 3.5 to 3.51 in a morning.  But I had the latest firmware, swxcr, sw
disks/CDs, release notes, etc all handy at the start.

Hope this helps

         /John Henning
          CSD Performance Group
          Digital Equipment Corporation
          Speaking for myself, not Digital
          henning@zko.dec.com





